          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you your personal risk, and I can't say how guidance uses family history to decide on a statin. The search did not return a passage that answers that.\n\n**What the retrieved material says about family history:** The general clinical reference on coronary artery disease lists \"a family history of premature coronary disease\" among the factors that contribute to the condition. It notes that age and sex cannot be changed, while the other factors can, which is why a risk estimate can guide action. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.) The passage does not say what age counts as \"premature,\" so I can't confirm whether your father's age at his heart attack fits that label.\n\n**What the search did not cover:** The statin guidance I searched for did not come back in usable form. I have no retrieved passage on how family history feeds into statin decisions, so I won't guess at it.\n\n**What you could discuss with a doctor or nurse:**\n- What your father's heart attack means for your own risk, given your age, blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, weight, and smoking history.\n- Whether a blood test for cholesterol would be useful for you.\n- Whether family history changes how your risk is estimated, and whether a statin is worth considering.\n- If your father's cholesterol or any inherited heart condition was ever tested, ask whether that information is relevant to you.\n\nIf you have chest pain, pressure, or tightness, shortness of breath, pain spreading to your arm, jaw or back, or sudden sweating or nausea, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
